.TH CJSON_DICTIONARY_SET 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cjson_dictionary_set \- set or replace a key in an object node
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cjson.h>

ccol_retval_t cjson_dictionary_set(cjson obj, const char *key, cjson child);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Sets the value for key in obj, or replaces it.  When a child is
already stored under key, the library deep-frees it and puts the new
child in its place in one step that cannot fail, so a failed insert
never leaves the slot dangling.  On success the object owns child and
the caller must not free it.

Members stay in insertion order: a new key goes after every other
member, and a replaced key keeps its place.  See
cjson_dictionary_first(3).

Each return code carries one ownership rule, the same as for
cjson_list_push:

  ccol_invalid_args always means that the call refused its arguments
  and touched nothing.  child is exactly as it was before the call,
  and whoever owned it before (the caller, or the container it is
  attached to) owns it and has to destroy it.

  Any other code that is not ccol_success (ccol_not_enough_memory,
  ccol_container_full) always means that ownership passed to this
  call and the call has deep-freed child, so freeing child again is
  a double free.

child must not be attached to a list or dictionary parent, with one
exception: you can pass back the exact node that is stored under key,
as in cjson_dictionary_set(obj, k, cjson_dictionary_get(obj, k)).
That call does nothing, returns ccol_success and leaves obj as it is.

These are the cases that give ccol_invalid_args: child is NULL; child
is attached somewhere else, under another key or in another
container; child is obj itself; obj is NULL or not a
CJSON_DICTIONARY; key is NULL or not valid UTF-8.  One more case is
refused: obj is reachable when you descend from child.  Attaching
child would then make obj a new ancestor of child while obj is also a
descendant of child, which is a cycle.

When memory is low the library cannot always finish that last check.
It doesn't risk an undetected cycle in that case: it returns
ccol_not_enough_memory, and the call deep-frees child just as it does
when the insert itself runs out of memory.

A set, a get and a remove take constant expected time, also when an
untrusted peer chooses the keys.  The object hashes its keys with a
secret seed that the library picks once per process, and when keys
collide it switches to a hash keyed with a secret 128-bit key; see
cjson_parse(3).

Every key in the tree is valid UTF-8, and that is what lets
cjson_serialize() write keys out unchanged.  A key that is not valid
UTF-8 is refused instead of stored; cjson_create_string(3) applies
the same rule to string values.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
obj    The target node, which must be a CJSON_DICTIONARY.
key    Null-terminated key, which must be valid UTF-8.  The library
       stores a copy of it.
child  The value node.  It must not be attached anywhere else and
       must not contain obj in its own subtree (see above).
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success, ccol_invalid_args, ccol_not_enough_memory, or
ccol_container_full when obj has reached its maximum element count.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A node that cjson_get(), cjson_list_get() or cjson_dictionary_get()
returns is a borrowed reference into an existing tree, and the caller
doesn't own it.  Never pass such a node as child, unless it is the
value stored under key (see above).  Call cjson_clone() first to get
an independent copy that is safe to store somewhere else.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_dictionary_get (3),
.BR cjson_dictionary_first (3),
.BR cjson_create_dictionary (3),
.BR cjson_create_string (3),
.BR cjson_list_push (3),
.BR cjson_clone (3)
